import json
from pathlib import Path
sweep=[json.loads(l) for l in Path('results.jsonl').read_text().splitlines()]
confirmed=json.loads(Path('confirmation-summary.json').read_text())
def find(mode,internal,leaves,rr,phase='warm'):
 return next(r for r in confirmed if r['navigation']==mode and r['internal_beam']==internal and r['leaf_beam']==leaves and r['rerank_factor']==rr and r['phase']==phase)
selected=[('Existing reader','1bit-4bit',256,256,8),('4-bit query','4bit-4bit',256,240,4),('FP query: fewer leaves','4bit-fp',256,192,8),('FP query: less reranking','4bit-fp',256,240,4)]
lines=['# Four-bit reader centroid experiment','','## Recommendation','','Use four-bit centroids with an FP32 query for the next reader experiment. They improve navigation enough to reduce the leaf beam by 25% at approximately 94.4% final recall without materially changing warm latency. A different setting halves full-precision reranking and lowers cold filesystem reads by 39%, with a 4.7% increase in warm latency. The four-bit-query implementation has almost the same accuracy but costs substantially more CPU time.','','## Measured operating points at approximately 94.5% recall','','The table averages three runs of the same 100 exact L2 queries on the saved 40M, 1024-dimensional Wikipedia index. All rows retain all root children and 256 nodes at the next internal level. “Reachable” means the fraction of exact neighbors present in the selected leaves before posting scoring and reranking. “Reranked” counts vectors whose original FP32 values are used for final distances.','','| Setting | Leaves | Codes scanned | Vectors reranked | Reachable recall | Final recall@100 | Warm mean ms | Cold mean ms | Cold filesystem MiB/query |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for label,mode,internal,leaf,rr in selected:
 r=find(mode,internal,leaf,rr);cold=find(mode,internal,leaf,rr,'cold')
 lines.append(f"| {label} | {leaf} | {r['codes_scanned']:,.0f} | {r['vectors_reranked']:.0f} | {r['reachable_recall']:.2%} | {r['recall_at_100']:.2%} | {r['latency_ms']:.2f} | {cold['latency_ms']:.2f} | {cold['filesystem_read_bytes_per_query']/2**20:.2f} |")
lines+=['','The 192-leaf FP-query setting scans 24.9% fewer posting codes. Its filesystem reads remain almost unchanged because it still fetches 800 original vectors for reranking. Fewer leaves alone do not guarantee fewer storage reads when reranking dominates.','','The 240-leaf FP-query setting scans 6.1% fewer posting codes, reranks 50% fewer vectors, and reduces measured filesystem reads from 724.63 to 441.16 MiB/query. Cold latency falls from 938.80 to 566.54 ms. These local reads do not establish a production network-latency improvement.','','## Operating points near 90% recall','','With 64 internal nodes retained, the existing path needs 128 leaves and 800 reranked vectors to reach 90.05% recall. Four-bit centroids with an FP32 query reach 90.28% using 112 leaves and 400 reranked vectors. Codes scanned fall from 190,524 to 167,056. Warm mean latency falls from 17.47 to 14.02 ms; cold mean latency falls from 1,004.73 to 554.17 ms. Cold filesystem reads fall from 722.44 to 437.22 MiB/query.','','## Leaf reachability across beam widths','','Each cell is the percentage of exact top-100 neighbors present in selected leaves. Reranking cannot recover neighbors outside those leaves. The internal beam is the number of nodes retained before choosing leaves.','','| Internal beam | Leaf beam | Existing 1-bit centroid / 4-bit query | 4-bit centroid / 4-bit query | 4-bit centroid / FP32 query |','|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for internal in [64,256]:
 for leaf in [8,16,32,64,128,256]:
  values=[]
  for mode in ['1bit-4bit','4bit-4bit','4bit-fp']:
   r=next(r for r in sweep if r['navigation']==mode and r['internal_beam']==internal and r['leaf_beam']==leaf and r['rerank_factor']==8 and r['phase']=='warm')
   values.append(f"{r['reachable_recall'][-1]:.2%}")
  lines.append(f"| {internal} | {leaf} | {' | '.join(values)} |")
lines+=['','Increasing centroid precision gives a modest gain rather than a complete change in navigation accuracy. At the wider internal beam and 128 leaves, reachable recall rises from 91.60% to 92.98%. Changing the query from four bits to FP32 changes that four-bit-centroid result from 92.90% to 92.98%; centroid precision accounts for most of the accuracy gain on this query set.','','## Why the four-bit query path is slower','','This implementation unpacks each packed centroid and forms four bit planes during a distance calculation. Bit planes group equal-position bits so weighted population counts can estimate the dot product of two quantized vectors. The FP32-query path instead decodes a small stack chunk and computes a float dot product. At the matched 240-leaf configuration, navigation takes roughly 48.6 ms with a four-bit query and 10.5 ms with an FP32 query. This comparison measures the implemented kernels; it does not prove that every optimized four-bit-query kernel must be slower. Preparing centroid bit planes once is a possible follow-up.','','## Memory, compatibility, and reproducibility','','Logical navigation code payload increases from 3.85 to 14.02 MiB for this tree, adding approximately 10.17 MiB. The experiment derives four-bit codes once from the saved original centroids and holds them in reader memory. It retains the old one-bit codes too. These payload numbers describe a replacement of stored codes; they are not measurements of total reader memory or persisted checkpoint size. Setup and code preparation are excluded from query latency.','','The source checkpoint remains unchanged. The experiment changes navigation scoring only; posting-list codes remain one bit and final reranking uses original FP32 vectors. Reader modes are selected through the new read-only benchmark. There is no persisted format migration in this branch.','','Branch: https://github.com/chroma-core/chroma/tree/codex/reader-centroid-precision','','Measured source commit: `d4d8c7b6ea7aa1b2d99313733622caae8fbcc02d`. The saved index is `/mnt/data/wikipedia-40m-baseline-20261009`. Exact ground truth is `/mnt/benchmark-source/wikipedia-40m-current/wikipedia_1m_40m_ground_truth.parquet`, SHA-256 `d4863b7df4213fb44f007180c99f5f65c14d19573d0af6473e2028602dcbdfdf`. All index file paths, sizes, inode numbers, and modification times match their pre-benchmark manifest.','','The initial sweep has 360 cold/warm rows. Confirmation measurements repeat selected configurations three times. Queries run concurrently with 32 workers on the existing r6id.16xlarge node, using a 1 GiB block cache. “Cold” clears reader posting data and reader block pins; it does not flush the provider cache or OS file cache. Filesystem bytes come from process read counters over a concurrent 100-query batch. They include full block reads served by the OS cache and are neither physical-disk bytes nor production network bytes. Mode order is fixed, and the 100-query set is small; production conclusions need a larger query sample and network-backed storage.','','Six quantization tests and 27 integration test executions pass. All index targets compile. Strict Clippy passes across all targets and features. The primary repository checkout is unchanged.','','Files: `results.jsonl` and `full-output.txt` contain the initial sweep; `confirmation/` contains repeated runs; `CONFIRMATION.md` contains their aggregate table; `command.txt`, `run-remote.sh`, and `run-confirmation.sh` record commands and input paths.','']
Path('REPORT.md').write_text('\n'.join(lines))
p=Path('STATUS.md');text=p.read_text();text=text.replace('The EC2 experiment is running against','The EC2 experiment is completed against');text+='\nThe initial 360-row sweep and repeated confirmation runs are complete. `REPORT.md` summarizes the results and limitations. All saved index files match their pre-run manifest.\n';p.write_text(text)
print('Wrote REPORT.md')
